window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_b1-13034c13b6497d66"]={"key":"ny_b1-13034c13b6497d66","group":"ny_b1","uid":"13034c13b6497d66","rank":89,"title":"wexcombe spares support decision memo","occupation":"buyers_purchasing_agents","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_b1","prompt":"You are Viktor Lindqvist, the senior buyer supporting Peatmoor Health Network's manufacturing-equipment portfolio, and Bianca Chakraborty has asked you to prepare the short memorandum that Amberfield Freight will use at its February supplier-review meeting. Amberfield Freight operates two bottling plants using case-packing lines supplied by Peatmoor, and its engineering manager, Bianca Calderon, has said that she wants spares to be easier to get before the service agreement renews. The account team needs one decision deck, not another discovery note: it must state what Amberfield appears to need, what Peatmoor is prepared to commit to, and what the renewal meeting is being asked to approve. You have no source pack or internal data to attach, so do not present invented consumption, failure, inventory, price, lead-time, or service-performance figures as facts; distinguish observations, assumptions, and decisions that still need confirmation. Put the finished file named `amberfield_spares_support_decision_memo.pptx` in the top level of the working directory, using exactly that filename. Keep it to no more than six slides and make it suitable to send to the customer after the meeting, with readable text and no speaker notes as a substitute for essential content. Please cover the following numbered requirements in a compact way: (1) frame the renewal decision and the operational problem in language that a maintenance planner can recognise; (2) compare the credible meanings of easier access, including plant-held or consigned stock, a dispatch-time promise, a defined critical-spares catalogue, and correction of part-number or as-built-documentation mismatches, without pretending that all four are included; (3) make one recommendation for a support package and explain the tradeoff between responsiveness, working capital, administrative effort, and control of obsolete or unsuitable parts; (4) state the customer-facing commitment in terms that can be checked, including its scope, ownership, exclusions, trigger or service window, and how exceptions or unavailable parts are communicated; (5) show how the recommendation would be confirmed with the two maintenance planners before the review, while keeping questions short enough to use in a working conversation; (6) separate purchasing and supplier-management responsibilities from engineering decisions about criticality, fit, approved substitutions, drawings, installation, and maintenance safety, and do not authorise work belonging to those functions; (7) give the meeting a clear decision to vote on, an accountable owner, a timing or effective-date assumption, and a small set of measurable review indicators; (8) include a brief comparison of the current or implied arrangement with the proposed arrangement, but label the baseline as unverified where the brief provides no evidence; (9) identify material risks such as stock becoming stranded, a part being misidentified, a promised response being mistaken for physical availability, or a planner using an unapproved substitute, with a proportionate control or escalation for each; (10) write for an audience in which the account and procurement readers understand commercial commitments but at least one customer reader does not know the purchasing terminology, so define only the few terms needed to make the decision clear rather than turning the deck into a tutorial; and (11) treat England and Wales as the applicable jurisdiction and state that the commercial and regulatory position is to be confirmed as at 31 January 2025, avoiding legal certainty or compliance claims that the available facts cannot support. Use the names Peatmoor Health Network and Amberfield Freight consistently, end on the single decision rather than a list of future possibilities, and ensure the recommendation, commitment, ownership, timing, risks, and vote wording agree throughout the deck.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `amberfield_spares_support_decision_memo.pptx` — A concise customer-facing spares-support decision memorandum for the February supplier-review meeting. (Must be no more than six slides.; Must contain the essential decision, recommendation, commitment, assumptions, risks, ownership, timing, and confirmation questions in the slides themselves.; Must make exactly one recommendation and end with the decision to be voted on.; Must be readable when rendered and suitable for external circulation.; Must not invent operational or commercial source data.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/amberfield_spares_support_decision_memo.pptx","bytes":42038,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/1c49e215db2ec7823e5413ac6fd4d03fd43893d462b238d9a4e1315919f69f25.pptx","sha256":"1c49e215db2ec7823e5413ac6fd4d03fd43893d462b238d9a4e1315919f69f25","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\nFEBRUARY REVIEW\nA decision on easier access\nto the right conveyor-line spare\nRenewal decision memorandum for Amberfield Freight’s two bottling plants\nThe operational problem\nWhen a case-packing conveyor needs a part, a planner needs an identified, suitable item and a clear route to obtain it — not a generic promise of ‘better spares’.\nDecision requested\nApprove one controlled catalogue-and-response package for the renewal, subject to the stated confirmations and technical authority.\nPrepared by Viktor Lindqvist, Senior Buyer  •  Customer-facing working draft\nCommercial and regulatory position: England and Wales — to be confirmed as at 31 January 2025.\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\nDECISION FRAME\n‘Easier access’ has four credible meanings\nBaseline is unverified: the brief gives no evidence of today’s stock, response, catalogue or documentation arrangement.\nPEATMOOR HEALTH NETWORK  /  AMBERFIELD FREIGHT\n2 / 6\nPlant-held / consigned stock\nParts physically at site; consigned means supplier ownership until use.\nFastest physical access • ties up cash / needs counts • can strand or age\nDispatch-time promise\nSupplier promises an action after a request, not that a part is held locally.\nResponsive coordination • low site stock • availability still uncertain\nCritical-spares catalogue\nA defined list of equipment-specific items to request and manage.\nBetter control • needs upkeep • avoids buying unsuitable items\nCorrect identity records\nResolve part-number, drawing or as-built mismatch before ordering.\nPrevents wrong item • administrative effort • does not create stock\nPROPOSED ARRANGEMENT\nSelect the catalogue + response elements. Do not include plant-held/consigned stock or a dispatch guarantee in this renewal package.\nNo consumption, failure, inventory, price, lead-time or performance data has been supplied; nothing above is a statement of current performance.\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\nRECOMMENDATION\nRecommend one bounded support package\nA controlled critical-spares catalogue with a defined supplier response — proportionate while evidence is incomplete.\nPEATMOOR HEALTH NETWORK  /  AMBERFIELD FREIGHT\n3 / 6\nWHY THIS BALANCE\nResponsiveness\nA named route and one-business-day update reduce ambiguity after a valid request.\nWorking capital & stock control\nAvoids committing cash or space to unvalidated site stock; catalogue review limits obsolete or unsuitable items.\nAdministration & technical suitability\nRequires controlled catalogue upkeep, but makes identification and technical approval visible rather than assumed.\nCUSTOMER-FACING COMMITMENT — PROPOSED FOR AGREEMENT\nScope\nThe two Amberfield Freight bottling plants; only catalogue entries jointly confirmed for the named case-packing lines.\nTrigger & service window\nFor a complete catalogue request sent to the agreed Peatmoor contact during business hours, Peatmoor confirms receipt and provides availability/status within 1 business day. Requests outside the window start next business day.\nOwnership & limits\nPeatmoor coordinates catalogue, request tracking and status. Amberfield supplies asset/part detail and authorises purchase. This is not plant-held stock, a dispatch promise, guaranteed physical availability, production restoration, technical fit or approval of a substitute.\nIf a catalogue item is unavailable, discontinued or technically uncertain, Peatmoor tells the named Amberfield planner in the status update and escalates fit/substitution to Amberfield engineering authority.\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\nCONFIRM BEFORE REVIEW\nTwo short planner conversations decide the catalogue scope\nUse the answers to confirm, narrow or defer items — not to turn the package into unbounded stock cover.\nPEATMOOR HEALTH NETWORK  /  AMBERFIELD FREIGHT\n4 / 6\nASK BOTH MAINTENANCE PLANNERS\n1  When a conveyor part is delayed, what stops, how soon, and at which plant?\n2  Which requests need a quick status update versus a part physically on site?\n3  Which item, asset or location details are hardest to identify when ordering?\n4  Do part numbers, drawings or as-built records ever conflict with the installed line?\n5  Which items require engineering confirmation before any substitute could be considered?\nDecision rule: high-consequence, repeatable, correctly identified items may enter the proposed catalogue after engineering confirmation; unresolved identity or technical questions stay out and are escalated.\nCLEAR BOUNDARIES\nPeatmoor purchasing / supplier management\nCoordinate supplier response, catalogue governance, commercial terms, order tracking and exception communication.\nAmberfield engineering / maintenance authority\nDecide criticality, fit, approved substitutions, drawings/as-built records, installation and maintenance safety.\nAmberfield planners\nProvide demand context and submit complete requests; do not use a substitute without technical approval.\nConfirmation assumption: both planners are consulted before the February supplier-review; no technical authority is delegated by this memorandum.\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\nCONTROLS & GOVERNANCE\nKeep the response commitment honest and reviewable\nThe controls below apply to the recommended catalogue-and-response package.\nPEATMOOR HEALTH NETWORK  /  AMBERFIELD FREIGHT\n5 / 6\nStock becomes stranded, obsolete or unsuitable\nNo site-stock commitment; catalogue owner reviews additions/removals with Amberfield engineering before renewal review.\nPart is misidentified or records mismatch\nHold request for asset/part verification; route drawing/as-built mismatch to engineering authority before order action.\nResponse is mistaken for physical availability\nStatus explicitly states available / unavailable / technical query; tell planner within the one-business-day update and agree escalation.\nUnapproved substitute is used\nNo substitution is supplied as approved; hold and seek Amberfield engineering’s written technical decision.\nREVIEW INDICATORS  •  accountable owner: Peatmoor Supplier Support Lead  •  monthly with Amberfield planners\n% complete catalogue requests receiving status within the service window  |  % requests needing identity correction  |  catalogue items with unresolved engineering decision  |  open unavailable/discontinued exceptions\nUse the monthly review to retain, amend or remove catalogue entries and to decide whether the support arrangement continues unchanged at the next renewal review.\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\nFEBRUARY SUPPLIER-REVIEW VOTE\nApprove the controlled catalogue-and-response package\nTHE SINGLE DECISION\nApprove renewal on the proposed terms for Amberfield Freight’s two bottling plants: a jointly confirmed critical-spares catalogue and Peatmoor availability/status update within 1 business day of each complete, in-window request — subject to the stated exclusions, engineering authority and planner confirmation.\nACCOUNTABLE OWNER\nPeatmoor Supplier Support Lead — coordinate implementation and monthly review.\nEFFECTIVE-DATE ASSUMPTION\nIf approved, effective on renewal signature after catalogue, contacts and engineering confirmations are recorded.\nEngland and Wales commercial and regulatory position to be confirmed as at 31 January 2025; this memorandum does not make a legal or compliance determination.\nPeatmoor Health Network  /  Amberfield Freight","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共6页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A PowerPoint file named amberfield_spares_support_decision_memo.pptx is present and opens as a valid presentation.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Submitted file and presentation metadata.","grader_hint":"Check exact filename, extension, and that the file can be opened or parsed as PPTX."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck contains no more than six slides.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Slide count.","grader_hint":"Inspect presentation slide count."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Peatmoor Health Network and Amberfield Freight are used consistently as the principal organisations.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Visible slide text.","grader_hint":"Search visible text and check that organisation names are not replaced by inconsistent alternatives."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies the February supplier-review or renewal decision context.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening or decision slide.","grader_hint":"Look for February review/renewal context."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The operational problem is expressed as difficulty obtaining suitable conveyor-line spares, rather than as a generic procurement issue.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Problem framing.","grader_hint":"Judge whether a maintenance planner would recognise the operational consequence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The memorandum distinguishes at least four plausible interpretations of easier access: plant-held or consigned stock, a dispatch-time promise, a defined critical-spares catalogue, and corrected part-number or as-built documentation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Needs/options comparison.","grader_hint":"Each interpretation must be recognisable and materially distinct."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The four interpretations are compared on consequences such as speed, cash or working-capital exposure, control effort, and risk of unsuitable or obsolete stock.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison content.","grader_hint":"Require comparative reasoning, not merely four labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck makes exactly one preferred recommendation rather than presenting an unresolved menu.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation and closing decision.","grader_hint":"A primary package may contain components, but alternatives must not remain equally recommended."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation is justified by an explicit tradeoff between responsiveness and at least two of working capital, administration, stock control, or technical suitability.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"Check that the rationale explains why the selected balance is defensible."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The proposed support package has a clearly bounded scope covering what parts, locations, or service requests it applies to.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Commitment statement.","grader_hint":"Scope must be more specific than 'all spares' or 'better support'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The customer-facing commitment identifies which party owns each relevant action.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles or commitment wording.","grader_hint":"Look for distinct responsibility for supplier, customer, engineering, and purchasing actions where relevant."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The commitment includes a checkable service window, trigger, or response measure rather than an unbounded promise of ease or speed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Service commitment.","grader_hint":"A measurable or operationally testable condition is required; invented historical performance is not."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck states what the commitment does not cover, including an appropriate treatment of unavailable, discontinued, or technically unsuitable parts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope/exclusions and risk controls.","grader_hint":"Check that exclusions prevent the promise from being read as guaranteed physical availability or technical approval."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Exceptions or missed service conditions have a named communication or escalation path.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception handling.","grader_hint":"The path should identify who is told and what decision or follow-up is required."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck distinguishes a dispatch or response promise from guaranteed on-site or plant-held availability.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Commitment wording.","grader_hint":"Reject wording that collapses these materially different service concepts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies how the critical-spares scope would be established or confirmed without claiming that purchasing alone determines technical criticality.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Confirmation and boundary content.","grader_hint":"Look for joint or engineering-led confirmation of criticality."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck includes a concise set of questions for the two maintenance planners that would discriminate among the competing interpretations of easier access.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Planner-confirmation content.","grader_hint":"Questions should address actual demand, urgency, location, documentation, and technical approval without becoming a questionnaire."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The planner questions seek evidence about consequences of delay or downtime, not only preferences about stock.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Planner questions.","grader_hint":"Look for operational impact, urgency, or failure consequence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The planner questions test whether part-number, drawing, or as-built identification problems are contributing to access difficulty.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Planner questions.","grader_hint":"A question about identification or documentation mismatch must be present."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck states how answers from the planners will affect the proposed scope or decision before the review.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Confirmation plan.","grader_hint":"Require a decision rule or explicit linkage, not merely 'speak to planners'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Purchasing or supplier-management responsibilities are separated from engineering decisions about fit, criticality, approved substitutions, drawings, installation, or maintenance safety.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roles and boundaries.","grader_hint":"The boundary must be visible and operationally useful."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The memorandum does not overstep by approving a substitute, declaring a part technically suitable, or directing maintenance work without the responsible technical authority.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole deck.","grader_hint":"Assess whether technical uncertainty is referred rather than silently resolved."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The proposed arrangement addresses the risk of obsolete, stranded, excess, or unsuitable stock through ownership, review, return, replenishment, or other control logic.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk/control content.","grader_hint":"The control must be connected to the chosen package."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck addresses the risk that an incorrect part identity or documentation mismatch could cause the wrong item to be ordered or used.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk/control content.","grader_hint":"Require a verification or escalation control, not merely naming the risk."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck addresses the risk that a planner or operator could treat an unapproved substitute as acceptable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk/control content.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit technical approval or hold language."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck addresses the risk that an expedited response commitment could be mistaken for guaranteed stock or guaranteed restoration of production.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk/control content.","grader_hint":"Check for a clear limitation and communication approach."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies at least three measurable indicators suitable for reviewing whether the arrangement is working.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review measures.","grader_hint":"Indicators should be observable, such as response compliance, identification accuracy, fulfilment, or unresolved exceptions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each proposed indicator has an owner, review cadence, or defined use in deciding whether the arrangement should continue or change.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review measures.","grader_hint":"Measures without accountability or use are insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck names an accountable owner for implementing or coordinating the approved supplier-support arrangement.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision/action content.","grader_hint":"A function or named role is acceptable if accountability is clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck states an effective-date or implementation-timing assumption linked to the February renewal decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing content.","grader_hint":"Timing must explain what happens if approval occurs at the review."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies what must be confirmed before the commitment can be finalised, without presenting those unknowns as settled facts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumptions and open points.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit labelling of assumptions, dependencies, or confirmation items."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck compares the unverified current or implied arrangement with the proposed arrangement and labels the baseline appropriately.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Current/proposed comparison.","grader_hint":"Do not award if the deck invents a current-state metric."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The recommendation and customer-facing commitment are consistent with the stated risks and controls.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Cross-slide internal coherence.","grader_hint":"Check whether controls actually mitigate risks created by the selected package."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The vote wording asks for approval of one bounded package or commitment, not a general endorsement of better service.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Closing slide.","grader_hint":"The decision must be specific enough for meeting participants to vote."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck ends on the single decision to be voted on rather than on a list of unresolved next steps or multiple future options.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final slide.","grader_hint":"A short implementation condition may accompany the vote, but the ending must resolve to one call."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The wording is understandable to a customer reader who does not know purchasing terminology, while retaining enough precision for procurement and account readers.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole deck.","grader_hint":"Judge clarity, selective definition of terms, and absence of unexplained commercial shorthand."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck is not turned into a tutorial, maintenance manual, or system click-through; it remains a decision memorandum.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole deck.","grader_hint":"Assess whether detail serves the decision rather than teaching general purchasing or maintenance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies England and Wales as the applicable jurisdiction and states that the position is to be confirmed as at 31 January 2025.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional note.","grader_hint":"Search visible slide text for both jurisdiction and date."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Any regulatory or legal language is appropriately qualified and does not claim unsupported certainty, certification, insurance, or compliance status.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Jurisdictional and commitment wording.","grader_hint":"Reward cautious scoping and confirmation language."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck does not present invented consumption, failure, price, stock, lead-time, or historical performance figures as verified facts.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"All visible content.","grader_hint":"Numbers may be illustrative or proposed targets only when clearly labelled as such."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any quantitative target or service threshold is paired with its scope, trigger, and limitation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Targets and commitment wording.","grader_hint":"A bare number without applicability conditions does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck is professionally usable in a renewal meeting: hierarchy is clear, text is legible, and each slide has a discernible purpose.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered presentation.","grader_hint":"Render slides and judge readability at normal presentation scale."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The deck demonstrates defensible professional judgement by selecting a proportionate support model despite incomplete information.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation, assumptions, and tradeoff rationale.","grader_hint":"A technically polished but noncommittal or unsupported recommendation should not pass."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Terminology, recommendation, commitment, ownership, timing, risk controls, review measures, and vote wording remain mutually consistent across slides.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole presentation.","grader_hint":"Trace the selected package through every section and note contradictions or scope drift."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"A manufacturing buyer is preparing a customer-facing renewal decision memorandum about spare-parts support for conveyor equipment. The artifact must convert an ambiguous service request into a bounded, vote-ready recommendation without making unsupported engineering or contractual claims.","difficulty_sources":["deciding what 'easier to get' means when several service models are plausible","reconciling the needs of a mixed-background audience without turning the memorandum into training","drawing a defensible boundary between purchasing commitments and engineering or maintenance ownership","synthesizing a single actionable recommendation while distinguishing known facts from assumptions and items requiring confirmation"],"authoring_plan":{"purpose":"Construct a compact, customer-facing normative decision memorandum that turns an ambiguous request for easier spares access into one bounded support commitment while preserving technical and legal boundaries.","reference_files":[],"gold_design":{"solver_feasible_content":["Use a four-option interpretation of easier access and select one package, such as a controlled critical-spares catalogue combined with a defined supplier response commitment, while explaining why full consignment or an unbounded availability promise is not proportionate without evidence.","Label all baseline conditions, planner inputs, commercial assumptions, and proposed targets distinctly because no source data exists.","Include concise planner questions that establish failure consequence, urgency, location, identification quality, and demand pattern.","Assign procurement or supplier-management ownership for catalogue coordination, order/response tracking, and exception communication, while reserving technical approval for engineering or maintenance authority.","Include controls for obsolete or stranded stock, incorrect identity, unapproved substitution, and confusion between dispatch and availability.","State England and Wales and the 31 January 2025 confirmation date with qualified language rather than asserting legal conclusions.","Close with one vote-ready recommendation and a practical effective-date assumption."],"professional_judgement_constraints":["The recommendation must balance responsiveness, working capital, administrative effort, and technical suitability.","The package must not silently combine every possible service promise.","The deck must make the commitment checkable without inventing historical figures.","A customer reader must be able to understand the decision without receiving a general purchasing tutorial.","The boundary with engineering and maintenance must be explicit and must not authorise technical choices."],"intended_verdict":"A strong solution makes one proportionate recommendation, records a bounded and measurable supplier position, identifies what the maintenance planners must confirm, distinguishes commercial ownership from technical authority, qualifies unknowns and jurisdictional statements, and remains coherent and readable within six slides."},"rubric_derivation":"R01-R02 verify the single deliverable and hard space cap. R03-R05 verify the scenario framing. R06-R15 assess synthesis of the four interpretations into a bounded service commitment. R16-R20 assess the unresolved planner evidence and how it changes scope. R21-R26 assess professional boundary and risk controls. R27-R35 assess execution, accountability, timing, and the single vote. R36-R39 assess audience and jurisdictional discipline. R40-R41 prevent unsupported quantitative claims while allowing clearly labelled proposed targets. R42-R44 assess usability, judgement, and internal coherence."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"supplied_template"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"spares_support_decision_deck","filename":"amberfield_spares_support_decision_memo.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"A concise customer-facing spares-support decision memorandum for the February supplier-review meeting.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be no more than six slides.","Must contain the essential decision, recommendation, commitment, assumptions, risks, ownership, timing, and confirmation questions in the slides themselves.","Must make exactly one recommendation and end with the decision to be voted on.","Must be readable when rendered and suitable for external circulation.","Must not invent operational or commercial source data."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"decision_memo","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"buyers_purchasing_agents","sector":"manufacturing","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":3.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have a manufacturing procurement professional and a presentation-quality reviewer independently inspect the rendered deck against the rubric, then reconcile disagreements about judgement items.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No source data is supplied, so factual historical performance cannot be verified; the rubric therefore rewards explicit assumptions and prohibits unsupported factual claims.","Several service models could be professionally defensible. Scoring should focus on bounded reasoning, internal coherence, and risk-aware commitment rather than one predetermined commercial package.","Offline review can assess general England-and-Wales qualification language but not provide live legal validation."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:14:05+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:14:05+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:14:05+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:14:05+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":false,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"instruction 与 rubric criterion 存在 8-gram 逐字重叠: ['R01', 'R06', 'R32', 'R38']","checked_at":"2026-08-20T19:14:05+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_b1/runs/13034c13b6497d66","declared_deliverables":["amberfield_spares_support_decision_memo.pptx"]};
